<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Federation deploy sweep (ADR-0103 D8) — Runner LaunchAgent, polling every 600s.

  poga-host: Runner

  This is a TEMPLATE. `deploy/install-deploy-sweep.sh` fills the __PLACEHOLDER__ tokens
  from the local machine and installs the concrete plist to ~/Library/LaunchAgents (never
  committed — it carries machine-specific absolute paths, P3).

  WHAT IT DOES. Compares every system in deploy/registry.json against its newest semver
  tag on origin and deploys anything newer, through exactly the same code path as
  `poga deploy <system>`. A version landed and tagged is running by the next sweep,
  with the operator reading only the outcome.

  WHY IT IS A LaunchAGENT AND NOT A DAEMON, for a different reason than the adopt-runner.
  The adopt-runner needs the Aqua session for the login keychain. This one does not touch
  `claude` at all (ADR-0103 D4 — the runner is plain code), so it has no keychain
  dependency and would work headless. It is an Agent because the SERVICES IT RESTARTS are
  LaunchAgents in the user's GUI domain: `launchctl kickstart gui/<uid>/<label>` has to be
  issued from a context that can reach that domain.

  SUPERSEDED by the mail-poller trigger (WI-0316, ADR-0103 amendment 2026-09-13):
  `curate/mail-poller.py` calls the runner's `unattended` verb on its own schedule, so
  this unit is no longer how the sweep runs. Kept rather than deleted because deleting a
  template does not retire a job already bootstrapped on a machine. (The verb is spelled
  here without its leading dashes on purpose: this is an XML comment, and a double hyphen
  inside one makes the whole plist unparseable. The suite caught exactly that.)

  DO NOT INSTALL THIS UNTIL the on-demand verb has deployed successfully twice and a
  rollback has been drilled (ADR-0103 D9). An unattended deployer whose failure path has
  never run is a deployer nobody should trust — the whole point of the smoke gate and the
  rollback is that they work, and "it has never been tested" is not evidence of that.

  Fails safe by construction: a smoke failure restarts nothing, a verify failure rolls
  back and re-verifies the old version, and either way it authors a comms/ blocked note.
  A sweep with nothing newer to deploy is a no-op that writes only a checked_at stamp.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.federation.deploy-sweep</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>__PYTHON__</string>
        <string>__FED_ROOT__/deploy/runner.py</string>
        <string>--sweep</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>__FED_ROOT__</string>

    <!-- launchd hands jobs a minimal PATH. git, uv and flox all live outside it, and a
         deploy that cannot find its dependency tool fails as a shell error rather than a
         named refusal — the WI-0187 lesson, paid for twice in one session.

         THE LIST BELOW IS NOT THE WHOLE PATH, and must not be read as it.
         `render_unit_plist` PREPENDS the rendering machine's `~/.local/bin` when it fills
         the tokens above. A member's contract may resolve its apply verb on PATH by
         design (ADR-0103 D8 shape 2) and that is where the operator installs those verbs:
         a real member's apply verb was there once, this unit could not see it,
         and the release stalled until a human ran the apply. The directory is not spelled
         out here on purpose — `~` is a different place on every machine and a committed
         `/Users/...` is WI-0132's defect, the same one the contract avoids.

         `install-*.sh` renders this same file with a plain `sed` over a fixed token list
         and does NOT add it, so a unit bootstrapped that way carries exactly the list
         below until a cutover re-renders it. -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <key>PYTHONUNBUFFERED</key>
        <string>1</string>
    </dict>

    <!-- Every 10 minutes, per the 2026-09-02 consultant brief's addendum.

         Was nightly at 04:30. That cadence made the sweep a BACKSTOP: a tag pushed
         at 10:00 did not deploy until the following morning, so the only way to see
         a change land the same day was to ask someone at the Runner to run it — the
         machine-bound handoff the 09-03 brief asks us to count as a defect, arriving
         through the schedule rather than through the code.

         A 600s poll is safe here precisely because the sweep is not the promotion
         event: ADR-0103 D2 makes the TAG the promotion, and the sweep only notices
         one. Polling more often therefore shortens the delay between a deliberate
         act and its effect; it does not create additional promotions. RunAtLoad
         stays false below for the same reason, and that reasoning is unchanged. -->
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>600</integer>

    <!-- Deliberately false. A deploy sweep that fires on every login turns "I rebooted"
         into a promotion event, which is the accidental-promotion failure ADR-0103 D2
         exists to prevent, arriving through the back door. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>__FED_ROOT__/.session-state/deploy-sweep.launchd.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>__FED_ROOT__/.session-state/deploy-sweep.launchd.err.log</string>

    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
